# Normalize every text file to LF in the repository, and check it out as LF
# everywhere.  This matters for more than tidiness: the ported build scripts
# (`ports/**/*.sh`) must run under Git Bash and under Linux CI alike, and
# `docs/项目状态与恢复指南.md` has to stay diffable for the recovery steps it
# documents.  A CRLF checkout would break both.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary artefacts: never touch their bytes, never try to diff them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.wasm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary

# The vendored official SDK is committed byte-for-byte, because the recipes under
# `ports/artemis/reverse/` assert byte-identity and export-surface equality against
# exactly these files.  `Copyright.txt` in that tree is CRLF, so the blanket
# `text=auto eol=lf` above would rewrite it on commit and break the identity the
# recipes check.  Nothing in this subtree is ours to normalise.
ports/artemis/vendor/** -text -diff

# The ONS engine files committed under `packages/runtime-ons/assets/` are build
# artefacts, not sources, and their exact bytes are pinned by sha256 in
# `ports/lib/engine-artifacts.json`.  `onsyuri.html` leaves the Emscripten build with
# CRLF line endings (582 of them), and `* text=auto eol=lf` above stored the LF form
# instead -- so the blob was 23,817 B where the ledger, the release asset and the
# build all say 24,399 B, and a fresh Linux checkout handed the aggregate package
# (which vendors this directory) different bytes than the ONS tarball (which takes the
# freshly built copy).  Store them verbatim: an artefact's bytes are the thing being
# pinned, so line-ending normalisation here is not tidiness, it is corruption.
packages/runtime-ons/assets/** -text -diff
